Ideas borrowed, with thanks

The Company memory — a git-backed markdown wiki with a derived index — follows the
design of ai-memory by Fabio Akita, which is MIT licensed:

    https://github.com/akitaonrails/ai-memory

    MIT License
    Copyright (c) Fabio Akita and the ai-memory contributors

What was taken is the shape rather than the code: markdown as the source of truth,
an index that is derived and can always be rebuilt from the files, capture with no
LLM in it, aging, and claim-once handoffs. TinyCorp's implementation is its own and
depends on nothing from that project — see docs/company-memory.md. The credit is
here because the thinking was theirs first.

An Employee's own notes — an append-only log with a fixed-size wake, assembled
from summaries over aligned power-of-two ranges — follow the design of OptMem by
Victor Taelin:

    https://github.com/VictorTaelin/OptMem

That repository carries no licence, so the idea is all that was taken: no code, no
prompt text, and no file from it is in this one. What was taken is the shape — a
log that is only ever appended to, a tree of summaries that is a rebuildable cache
over it, and a session start that reads the whole history inside a budget that does
not grow. TinyCorp's implementation is its own; see docs/company-memory.md. The
credit is here because the thinking was theirs first.

Lean builder — the Company add-on that has every Employee climb a ladder before it
writes code, and stop at the first rung that holds — is ponytail's ladder, by
Dietrich Gebert, which is MIT licensed:

    https://github.com/DietrichGebert/ponytail

    MIT License
    Copyright (c) 2026 DietrichGebert

What is in the app is our own wording of the seven rungs and the never-cut list, not
a copy: src/shared/build-style.ts is the whole of it, and the card that offers it says
"From ponytail · MIT" where the boss can read it. The verbatim ruleset is vendored
separately, below, and only the benchmark reads that. The credit is here because the
ladder was theirs first, and because the numbers the card shows — 28% fewer tokens,
32% less cost, 29% fewer lines — are ponytail's ladder being measured, not ours.

Caveman — the Company add-on that has every Employee answer the boss in few words —
is caveman's idea, by Julius Brussee:

    https://github.com/JuliusBrussee/caveman

That project's licence is split: its skill instructions are MIT, and its engine and
its proxy are BSL-1.1, which is not an open source licence. Nothing of either is
here. No code, no prompt text and no file from it is in this repository, and neither
the engine nor the proxy was read for it: what was taken is the idea, that an agent
can answer the person reading in fragments while everything a machine or a record
needs stays whole. TinyCorp's wording is its own — src/shared/caveman.ts is the whole
of it, three intensities and what it never touches — and the card that offers it says
"From caveman · MIT" where the boss can read it. The card shows no number, because at
the time of writing nobody has measured it; tools/bench/boss.mjs is the arm that will.
The credit is here because the idea was theirs first.

